Electronic filing of the individual income tax return isn't optional in Sri Lanka anymore — it's mandatory under section 113(1B) of the Inland Revenue Act No. 24 of 2017. Paper returns for individuals are no longer accepted, so understanding the IRD's e-Services portal (built on RAMIS, the Revenue Administration Management Information System) is a practical necessity, not a nice-to-have.

Here's the full process, step by step.

![Tax forms, a calculator, and a pen laid out on a desk next to a cup of coffee](https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1554224155-6726b3ff858f?q=80&w=1600&auto=format&fit=crop)

## Before you start: what to have ready

Filing goes faster — and with fewer errors — if you gather everything first rather than hunting for documents mid-form.

- Your Taxpayer Identification Number (TIN)
- Your IRD e-Services username and password (register at [eservices.ird.gov.lk](https://eservices.ird.gov.lk) if you haven't already)
- APIT/PAYE certificates from every employer during the year
- Withholding tax (AIT) certificates for interest, dividends, or rent received
- Records of foreign-currency income and any tax already paid overseas
- Proof of qualifying payments (donations, solar installation costs, and similar reliefs)
- Business income and expense records, if you're self-employed or freelancing

*Source: [TaxWise.lk, "How to File Your Income Tax Return in Sri Lanka — 2025/2026 Step-by-Step Guide"](https://taxwise.lk/blog/how-to-file-income-tax-return-sri-lanka).*

## Step 1: Register for a TIN, if you don't have one

If this is your first time filing, you'll need a Taxpayer Identification Number before you can do anything else. Registration is available online or at a regional IRD office. If you already have a TIN from a previous year, skip ahead.

## Step 2: Activate your e-Services account

Sign in at the IRD e-Services portal using your TIN and password. If you've never logged in before, you'll need to complete an initial account activation step tied to your TIN.

## Step 3: Open the Return of Income (IIT) for the correct year

Once logged in, navigate to the return-filing section and select the Individual Income Tax return for the relevant Year of Assessment — for example, YA 2025/2026 for income earned between 1 April 2025 and 31 March 2026. The portal also supports a simplified filing option for eligible individual taxpayers with straightforward income sources.

*Source: [IRD Opens Online Tax Return Filing for 2025/2026 Assessment Year — Lanka News Web](https://lankanewsweb.net/archives/231256/ird-opens-online-tax-return-filing-for-2025-2026-assessment-year/).*

## Step 4: Enter your income, by source

Work through each income schedule in turn:

1. Employment income — enter figures from your APIT/PAYE certificates.
2. Investment income — interest, dividends, and any AIT already withheld.
3. Rental income, if applicable.
4. Foreign-currency income, including any tax already paid overseas that may qualify for a credit.
5. Business or self-employment income, with allowable expenses.

## Step 5: Claim your reliefs and credits

This is where most of the return's real value sits. Declare your personal relief (LKR 1,800,000 for YA 2025/26), any qualifying payment reliefs, and every credit you're entitled to — APIT already withheld, AIT already withheld, and any foreign tax credit. Missing a credit here is the single most common way people overpay: the withholding already happened, but it only counts if you actually declare it on the return.

## Step 6: Review before you submit

Before submitting, re-check every schedule against your source documents. Once a return is submitted electronically, correcting it means filing an amendment — not simply re-editing the same form — so it's worth the extra ten minutes here.

## Step 7: Submit and pay any balance

Submit electronically through the portal. If the return shows a balance still owing after your credits, you can typically pay through the portal itself or through an approved bank channel.

![Someone signing a document with a pen, close up on the hand and paper](https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1450101499163-c8848c66ca85?q=80&w=1600&auto=format&fit=crop)

## Mistakes that slow this down every year

A few patterns show up repeatedly and are worth checking for specifically:

- **Forgetting a mid-year employer.** If you changed jobs during the year, you need APIT certificates from both employers, not just the most recent one.
- **Not declaring foreign income correctly.** Income paid in foreign currency has its own treatment and concession — folding it into ordinary employment income understates or overstates what you actually owe.
- **Missing AIT credits on interest income.** Banks withhold AIT automatically; if you don't declare it on the return, you don't get the credit for tax that's already been paid.
- **Waiting until the last week of November.** The IRD has explicitly flagged that portal traffic and technical issues spike right before the 30 November 2026 deadline for YA 2025/26 — filing even a week early avoids that entirely.

## Why this is easier with a live ledger

Every one of those mistakes comes from the same root cause: reconstructing a year of financial activity from memory and scattered documents in the days before a deadline. If your transactions were already recorded as they happened — the right account, the right credit, the right currency treatment — filling in the return schedules becomes a matter of reading numbers off a report rather than reconstructing a year from scratch. That's the specific gap Salli's ledger and tax engine are built to close: the numbers are already there, correctly categorised, months before the return is due.

